\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces This is the pi-model circuit involved in the basic ``loadflow'' that we solve at several steps of the procedure. In our calculations, Terminal 1 always represents the bus where both complex voltage and complex current (or, equivalently, complex voltage and P \& Q) are known. The complex voltage and current on terminal 2 are calculated through simple algebra (so this is not really a loadflow problem, strictly speaking). Notation: (tr) stands for \emph  {transmission} branch; (sh) stands for \emph  {shunt} admittance.}}{4}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:pimodel_LF}{{3}{4}{This is the pi-model circuit involved in the basic ``loadflow'' that we solve at several steps of the procedure. In our calculations, Terminal 1 always represents the bus where both complex voltage and complex current (or, equivalently, complex voltage and P \& Q) are known. The complex voltage and current on terminal 2 are calculated through simple algebra (so this is not really a loadflow problem, strictly speaking). Notation: (tr) stands for \emph {transmission} branch; (sh) stands for \emph {shunt} admittance}{figure.caption.4}{}}
